1
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:05,012
Theo: Welcome to Briefly, How It Works.

2
00:00:05,012 --> 00:00:08,103
I'm Theo, a computer-generated voice.

3
00:00:08,103 --> 00:00:10,852
Mira: And I'm Mira, also computer-generated.

4
00:00:10,852 --> 00:00:13,427
Today: what a compiler actually does.

5
00:00:13,427 --> 00:00:15,494
Theo: Good, because I write some code,

6
00:00:15,494 --> 00:00:17,999
press a button, and a program appears.

7
00:00:17,999 --> 00:00:19,715
What happens in between?

8
00:00:20,295 --> 00:00:22,236
Mira: A compiler is a translator.

9
00:00:22,236 --> 00:00:24,295
It takes the text you wrote and turns it

10
00:00:24,295 --> 00:00:26,860
into instructions a processor can run.

11
00:00:26,860 --> 00:00:29,068
First, it splits your text into small pieces

12
00:00:29,068 --> 00:00:32,456
called tokens: names, numbers, symbols.

13
00:00:32,456 --> 00:00:34,871
Theo: Like splitting a sentence into words.

14
00:00:34,871 --> 00:00:35,925
Mira: Exactly.

15
00:00:35,971 --> 00:00:38,668
Then it works out how those pieces fit together,

16
00:00:38,668 --> 00:00:41,824
and builds a tree that shows the structure of the program.

17
00:00:42,374 --> 00:00:43,795
Theo: And if I made a mistake?

18
00:00:43,795 --> 00:00:45,442
Mira: This is where you hear about it.

19
00:00:45,442 --> 00:00:47,922
The compiler checks the tree against the rules of

20
00:00:47,922 --> 00:00:50,797
the language, and reports what does not fit.

21
00:00:50,797 --> 00:00:52,443
Theo: And when everything fits?

22
00:00:52,443 --> 00:00:56,325
Mira: It can tidy the program, so it does the same job with less work.

23
00:00:56,325 --> 00:00:59,142
Then it writes out machine code for the CPU.

24
00:00:59,652 --> 00:01:01,623
Theo: So: split the text, build a tree,

25
00:01:01,623 --> 00:01:03,662
check it, tidy it, translate it.

26
00:01:03,662 --> 00:01:04,924
Mira: That's a compiler.

27
00:01:04,924 --> 00:01:06,293
Thanks for listening.
